<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="intellij-plugin" title="The IntelliJ IDEA plugin">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Needs IntelliJ IDEA 2026.2 or newer and the IDE's own Java plugin. Nothing publishes
            it, so you build it yourself:
            <a href="building-and-testing.topic">Building and testing</a>.</p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        The IntelliJ IDEA plugin shows you the class the build produces. It changes no bytes.
    </p>

    <table style="header-row">
        <tr><td>In the editor</td><td>What the plugin adds</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Completion</td><td>Merged members, extensions, selectors</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Red underlines</td><td>Six inspections and ten quick fixes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alt+Enter</td><td>Nine intentions, two write a handler</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alt+Insert</td><td>Weave Handler..., in the Generate menu</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gutter</td><td>Three markers, in both directions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inlays</td><td>The injected code, where it lands</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tool window</td><td>Weaves: every weave and holder in sight</td></tr>
    </table>

    <chapter title="What the inspections report" id="inspections">
        <p>
            Six, enabled at error level, under <control>Aether Weaver</control> in the
            inspection settings.
        </p>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="Selector does not bind to a target member">
                <p>
                    A <code>method</code> selector that does not parse. One that names no method
                    any target declares or inherits. A bare name where the targets have several
                    of them.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <code>AW1020</code>, <code>AW1021</code>, and the parser's own
                    <code>AW1015</code>, <code>AW1017</code> to <code>AW1019</code>: name a
                    method a target declares, spelled as
                    <a href="selector-grammar.topic">the selector grammar</a> accepts.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Weave declaration cannot work as written">
                <p>
                    A <code>@Weave</code> naming no target, or naming its targets as literals and
                    as strings at once. Also what a static weave may not hold: a
                    <code>@Shadow</code>, a <code>@Unique</code>, a non-static handler.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <code>AW1001</code>, <code>AW1002</code>, <code>AW1005</code>,
                    <code>AW1090</code>, <code>AW1091</code>: name the targets once, and keep
                    those three out of a static weave.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Handler signature does not fit the target">
                <p>
                    A handler that returns something. One whose parameters are not a prefix of
                    the target's, or whose callback names the wrong value type.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <code>AW1040</code>, <code>AW1041</code>, <code>AW1070</code>,
                    <code>AW1071</code>: declare it <code>void</code>, with the parameters and
                    the callback type <a href="handlers-and-callbacks.topic">Handlers</a> gives.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Weave member conflicts with the target">
                <p>
                    A <code>@Shadow</code> field typed differently from the target's. An
                    <code>@Accessor</code> or <code>@Invoker</code> landing on a member that
                    exists. A merged member colliding, or a handler the injected call cannot
                    reach.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <code>AW1031</code>, <code>AW1042</code>, <code>AW1080</code>,
                    <code>AW1095</code>: match the target's type, rename what collides, or make
                    the handler reachable.
                    <a href="weave-members.topic">Weave members</a> has the merge rules.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Extension declaration cannot work as written">
                <p>
                    A holder that is not final, is generic, or has a supertype. A contributed
                    method that is not static or is generic. A receiver that is missing, doubled,
                    not first, parameterised or <code>Object</code>. A contribution the receiver
                    already has.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Fourteen codes, all of them <code>AW13xx</code>: make the holder final, the
                    contribution static, and its first parameter the receiver.
                    <a href="extension-methods.topic">Extension methods</a> has the form.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="@At target cannot name an operation">
                <p>
                    A <code>target</code> written on a point that names a position. A point that
                    needs one and has none. A target whose owner has no such member.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <code>AW1043</code>, and the parser's codes where the target does not parse:
                    name a member the owner has, on a point that takes a target.
                    <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection points</a> lists them.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            The editor cannot invent a code. It carries the API's own selector parser and the
            framework's own <code>DiagnosticCode</code>.
            <a href="diagnostics-declaration.topic">AW1xxx</a> has the entry behind each one.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Where the editor shows less than the build" id="limits">
        <p>
            An inspection reports what the source shows. Three things belong to the build.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>The editor says less than the build, never something else. One corpus of wrong
                weaves runs through the annotation processor and through five of the six
                inspections. The two sets of codes are compared; the <code>@At</code> target
                inspection is left out.</p></li>
            <li><p>Which positions an injection point matched is decided against compiled bytes,
                so every site the plugin draws is a plan. The <control>Weaves</control> window
                says so in its footer.</p></li>
            <li><p>Nothing is numbered without a class file newer than its source.
                <control>Weave here</control> and <control>Weave Handler...</control> say why.
                Completion and the inspections fall back to the source in silence.</p></li>
        </list>

        <note>
            <p>One shape is the editor's alone. <code>AW1070</code> underlines a
                <code>cancel()</code> that passes no value, on a <code>ReturnableCallback</code>
                whose target returns one. The declaration is right; the call becomes
                <code>cancel(value)</code>.</p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Quick fixes" id="fixes">
        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>When the editor underlines</td><td>Quick fix</td><td>Code</td></tr>
            <tr><td>a non-static handler, static weave</td><td>Declare the handler static</td>
                <td><code>AW1005</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>a handler the call cannot reach</td><td>Make the handler and its weave public</td>
                <td><code>AW1042</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>parameters that are not a prefix</td><td>Adjust handler parameters to the target</td>
                <td><code>AW1040</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>a selector naming no such method</td><td>Change selector to an existing member</td>
                <td><code>AW1020</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>text that reads as a descriptor</td><td>Apply the selector parser's suggestion</td>
                <td><code>AW1017</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>a @Shadow field in a static weave</td><td>Replace @Shadow with an @Accessor pair</td>
                <td><code>AW1090</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>a @Shadow method in a static weave</td><td>Replace @Shadow with an @Invoker</td>
                <td><code>AW1090</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>a holder that is not final</td><td>Declare the extension class final</td>
                <td><code>AW1300</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>a contribution that is not static</td><td>Declare the contributed method static</td>
                <td><code>AW1301</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>a contribution naming no receiver</td><td>Mark the first parameter @Receiver</td>
                <td><code>AW1302</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>@Receiver on a later parameter</td><td>Move the @Receiver parameter first</td>
                <td><code>AW1303</code></td></tr>
        </table>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Intentions and generated handlers" id="intentions">
        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Intention</td><td>Offered on</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Weave here</td><td>a method body outside a weave</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Weave where this variable is live</td><td>a local in that same body</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Declare @Shadow for the target's member</td><td>a red reference in a weave</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Create a weave for this class</td><td>a top-level or nested class, no @Weave</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Create extension method '...' in ...</td><td>a red call on an extended type</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Qualify the selector's parameter types</td><td>a selector that writes a parameter list</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Convert selector to source form</td><td>a selector in descriptor form</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Convert the target to source form</td><td>an @At target in descriptor form</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Convert the target to descriptor form</td><td>an @At target naming one method</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            <control>Weave Handler...</control> generates the same handler from the Generate
            menu, in a weave whose targets resolve. It and the two Weave intentions each write one
            method, with imports rather than qualified names. Nothing is written where the
            selector, the type or the operation does not come back. A conversion re-resolves
            first, so a rewrite renames nothing.
        </p>

        <p>
            <control>Weave where this variable is live</control> offers the positions the
            variable can still be read at. The target has to carry a local variable table.
            <a href="locals.topic">Locals</a> has what <code>@Local</code> binds by.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Members that are not in the source" id="augmentation">
        <p>
            Two providers put on a class what the build gives it. Completion, navigation and
            Find Usages then see the woven class.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="On a weave's target">
                <p>
                    Every member a weave that dissolves merges in, its accessors and invokers, and
                    its handlers as private. A <code>@Shadow</code> is left off, and so is a member
                    whose name the target already has.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="On an extension's receiver">
                <p>
                    Contributed methods without their receiver parameter, and contributed
                    constants. A holder in the project and one in a jar arrive the same way. The
                    completion row carries a tail naming the class it comes from.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            No source calls a handler or assigns a shadow field. The unused-declaration
            inspection is told to leave both alone. An ordinary private method of a weave is
            still reported. So is a <code>@Shadow</code> method nothing calls.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Gutter, navigation and inlays" id="navigation">
        <p>
            A selector string works like a call site. <control>Go to declaration</control>,
            <control>Find Usages</control> and <control>Rename</control> all reach the member it
            names. A rename rewrites the name inside the literal and leaves the parameter list
            standing.
        </p>

        <p>
            Completion inside a selector offers the target's own methods and its merged
            members. Inherited methods are left out. Inside an <code>@At</code> target it offers
            the operations of the compiled method. Each row says how often that operation
            occurs. Name a class there and you get its members instead, without a count.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Three gutter markers">
                <p>
                    On a woven member, every handler reaching it, from this project and from a
                    dependency. On a handler carrying <code>@Inject</code> or
                    <code>@Redirect</code>, the members it weaves into. On an extension
                    declaration, the type that gains it.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Injected code, in place">
                <p>
                    A block inlay draws each handler above the line it lands on.
                    <control>Show Injected Code</control>, in the View menu and the editor popup,
                    collapses every one of them in every open project.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Injection-site hints">
                <p>
                    Read from the weave's side: an end-of-line hint on the annotation names the
                    lines of the target the injection reaches. A point that matches nothing shows
                    no hint.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The Weaves window">
                <p>
                    A snapshot on the right, rebuilt on demand: every weave with its targets and
                    handlers, every extension holder with its contributions. A row from the
                    project opens its declaration on a double-click.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What it reads, and what it needs" id="requirements">
        <p>
            Three sources, none of them the framework at run time.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="The weave manifest of every library">
                <p>
                    <code>META-INF/aether/weaves.json</code>, from each library root, jar or
                    directory. That is where the weaves and extensions a dependency ships come
                    from. A manifest that cannot be read is skipped in silence, and the result is
                    re-read when the dependencies change.
                    <a href="manifest-format.topic">The manifest reference</a> has the format.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The target's class file">
                <p>
                    Read whenever a declaration counts instructions rather than statements: any
                    <code>ordinal</code>, <code>shift</code> or <code>slice</code>. Read too
                    wherever the source has no body to walk, as a decompiled target has none.
                    Every feature that names an operation reaches for it.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Two indexes over the project">
                <p>
                    Weaves by target name and holders by receiver name, both read as text.
                    A target the indexer cannot read is kept rather than dropped. A weave being
                    typed never disappears from the file it applies to.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            <code>sinceBuild</code> is 262 and no <code>untilBuild</code> is declared, so a
            newer IDE is never refused. <code>./gradlew runIde</code> starts an IDE with the
            plugin and its sample project open.
            <a href="building-and-testing.topic">Building and testing</a> has that build.
        </p>
    </chapter>

